# Restore the character who is standing on a down staircase and climb down it.
# This is the oracle for inc-wcf, the false "The stair leads to <terrain>.
# Confirm unsafe action?" that fired on every descent. Read the result with
# tools/check_stair_warn.sh, which stages the save and asserts on what this
# session leaves behind. This file only plays the session.
#
#   tools/check_stair_warn.sh
#
# WHY A SAVED CHARACTER AND NOT A GENERATED ONE. The prompt lives in
# Portal::Enter (src/Feature.cpp), which only a player standing on a real down
# staircase can reach: '>' is Creature::Descend (src/Skills.cpp), and it looks
# for a T_PORTAL under the player's own feet. Wizard mode's 'Ascend / Descend
# Depth' calls MoveDepth directly and never enters the portal, so dive.keys
# cannot reach this code at all. A blind walk does not get there either --
# forty rounds of the overview map's own Tab-then-Run explore on six seeds
# found no down staircase, because the character stays boxed in by locked
# doors and deep water. The saved character is already on one.
#
# [b] is "Restore a Saved Game" on the Initial Choices menu (Game::StartMenu,
# src/Main.cpp). The character picker prints the name with a SPACE, not the
# underscore the file uses, so @choose asks for "Furious Fox".
#
# The two rests after '>' matter. A descent generates the arrival level, and
# the screen dumped in the same instant shows the message line and no map;
# two turns of game time let the arrival settle so the status line carries
# the new depth. That depth is how the check knows the descent really
# happened rather than being refused at a prompt.

b
@choose "Furious Fox"
@dump:loaded

>
.*2
@dump:descend

@quit
